 social media let's take a look at a  tweet from an indian user in  delhi now who writes uh that pakistanis  have become great fans of wing commander  abhinandan who spoke like a boss even  under the most trying and  intimidating conditions inside an enemy  country as a pow  after all statues are built for heroes  here's a response from a user  located in england with a pakistani take  on the exhibit saying  of course we love abhipai he really  enjoyed our fantastic tea and he didn't  say anything bad about pakistan  when we sent back through the land  border we can always invite him again  when he brings us another plane and  fancy's more  fantastic tea  all right words brewing with sarcasm on  both sides there for more on the story  we're joined by dw asia correspondent  gelani hi shazam good to see you it  seems like this exhibit really has  triggered strong reactions both in  india and in pakistan what do you make  of all this  that's right and it's a predictable  banter between  people on either side of the border  i was in pakistan when this episode took  place back in  february and i can tell you that the  mood was really grim in pakistan  after indian jets struck deep inside the  pakistani territory  but within 24 to 48 hours when pakistan  shot down  indian air jets and captured wing  commander abhinandan  the mood changed now at the time if you  remember pakistan took a moral high  ground and they said we will return  him and they did but they made sure to  play with the optics you know  they filmed him while in custody uh they  paraded him in front of live  television when he was handed over so  pakistan has tried to  make the most of this opportunity in  terms of propaganda war  and that continues to this day what do  you think pakistan's aim is here with  this exhibit is it precisely playing  with optics  it is and i mean the two countries are  in some ways a mirror image of each  other  it happens on the indian side too if you  look at the indian film 